<div id="" class="width-full maxw-full padding-bottom-9 padding-top-9 hds-color-mode-light color-mode-light bg-carbon-05 hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-featured-link">
    <div class="grid-container grid-container-block padding-x-0 padding-top-9 desktop:padding-top-0">
        <div class="grid-row flex-align-center">
            <div class="grid-col-12 desktop:grid-col-6 padding-x-3 desktop:padding-right-5 margin-bottom-5">
                <div class="label margin-bottom-2">NASA history</div>
                <h2 class="page-heading-md">How 11 Deaf Men Helped Shape NASA’s Human Spaceflight Program</h2>
                <p class="p-md">Before NASA could send humans to space, the agency needed to better understand the effects of prolonged weightlessness on the human body. So, in the late 1950s, NASA and the U.S. Naval School of Aviation Medicine established a joint research program to study these effects and recruited 11 deaf men aged 25-48 from Gallaudet College (now Gallaudet University). Today, these men are known to history as the “Gallaudet Eleven.”</p>
            
                <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/missions/project-mercury/how-11-deaf-men-helped-shape-nasas-human-spaceflight-program/" target="_self" class="button-primary button-primary-md" aria-label="Learn More about How 11 Deaf Men Helped Shape NASA’s Human Spaceflight Program">
